1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,876
A server reboots at three in the morning.

2
00:00:02,896 --> 00:00:06,469
When it comes back, the website is down, nobody can log in,

3
00:00:06,489 --> 00:00:08,478
and the cause is one small thing:

4
00:00:08,498 --> 00:00:12,071
a service that was running, but never set to start at boot.

5
00:00:12,541 --> 00:00:16,958
In this video: configure network services to start automatically at boot.

6
00:00:21,478 --> 00:00:24,061
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:24,531 --> 00:00:28,031
Configure network services to start automatically at boot.

8
00:00:28,501 --> 00:00:32,723
We'll check a service, enable it so it starts at boot, start it right now,

9
00:00:32,743 --> 00:00:35,990
and prove it listens on the network, even after a reboot.

10
00:00:43,510 --> 00:00:47,555
A network service is a program that waits for connections, on a port.

11
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:51,738
Two different questions: is it enabled, meaning, will it start at boot?

12
00:00:51,758 --> 00:00:56,098
And is it active, meaning, is it running right now? Both can be different.

13
00:01:03,568 --> 00:01:08,249
Enable creates the link that starts it at boot. Start runs it now.

14
00:01:08,269 --> 00:01:12,737
The option dash dash now does both in one command. Our example:

15
00:01:12,757 --> 00:01:16,369
rpcbind, a service that listens on port one eleven.

16
00:01:23,839 --> 00:01:29,278
Before the demo, silently, the lab makes sure rpcbind is disabled and stopped.

17
00:01:29,298 --> 00:01:31,307
This step changes the system.

18
00:01:39,827 --> 00:01:44,819
First, three services. Systemctl is enabled, Network Manager:

19
00:01:44,839 --> 00:01:49,585
enabled. Sshd, the remote login service: enabled. Rpcbind:

20
00:01:49,605 --> 00:01:52,543
disabled. It will not start at boot.

21
00:02:03,063 --> 00:02:05,817
Is it running now? Systemctl is active:

22
00:02:05,837 --> 00:02:10,939
inactive. And does anything listen on port one eleven? Ss shows sockets.

23
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:14,496
Dash t, T C P. Dash l, listening. Dash n, numbers.

24
00:02:14,516 --> 00:02:20,330
The pipe passes the result to grep, which keeps only lines with colon one one one.

25
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:21,326
Nothing found.

26
00:02:32,846 --> 00:02:36,912
Now fix it. Sudo systemctl enable, dash dash now, rpcbind.

27
00:02:36,932 --> 00:02:42,477
It prints the link it created, from the multi user target, to the service file.

28
00:02:42,497 --> 00:02:45,647
That link is what starts the service at boot.

29
00:02:56,167 --> 00:03:00,185
Check both questions. Is enabled: enabled. Is active:

30
00:03:00,205 --> 00:03:06,204
active. And now the socket check shows two listening lines, one for I P v four,

31
00:03:06,224 --> 00:03:09,480
one for I P v six, both on port one eleven.

32
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,436
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

33
00:03:24,956 --> 00:03:30,330
We reboot. Rpcbind is enabled, and it is active again. Nobody started it:

34
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:32,104
the system did, at boot.

35
00:03:40,624 --> 00:03:44,584
Your turn. Enable the service N F S server, and start it now.

36
00:03:44,604 --> 00:03:49,021
And do the opposite for cockpit dot socket: disable it, and stop it.

37
00:03:49,041 --> 00:03:51,761
Run the checker, reboot, and run it again.

38
00:04:00,231 --> 00:04:01,982
Try before you open the solution.

39
00:04:07,502 --> 00:04:12,474
Next up: restrict network access using firewalld and firewall-cmd.

40
00:04:12,944 --> 00:04:15,847
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
